<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Orinoco</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Orinoco River</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Orinoco is one of the longest rivers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a>, flowing about 2,140 kilometres from the Guiana Highlands through Venezuela and along the Colombian border to a vast delta on the Atlantic. Its plains, the llanos, were a battleground of the independence wars, and its town of Angostura served as Bolívar's base. Early explorers sought the legendary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-dorado">El Dorado</a> along its course.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/orinoco.jpg" alt="Orinoco" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: jkaiser · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Orinoco_Bridge.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the collapse of the First and Second Venezuelan Republics, Simón Bolívar rebuilt his forces in the <b>Orinoco</b> basin, where the Congress of Angostura gathered in February 1819.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gran-colombia">Gran Colombia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…Guatavita to a golden city and a lost kingdom, drew expeditions into the Llanos, the Amazon and the <b>Orinoco</b> for more than a century, among them Sir Walter Raleigh's voyage to Guiana in 1595, and treasure-hunters tried to drain Lake Guatavita from the sixteenth century to the early…</blockquote><a href="/empire/muisca-confederation">Muisca Confederation · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>European attempts to settle the Guiana coast between the <b>Orinoco</b> and the Amazon began in the early seventeenth century, when Dutch, English and French traders built short-lived posts along its rivers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/suriname">Suriname · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The traditional account, almost as old as Columbus and widespread in oral testimony, held that the Kalinago came from South America, from the <b>Orinoco</b> around 1200 CE, and conquered and displaced the earlier islanders, the Igneri.</blockquote><a href="/culture/kalinago">Kalinago · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>One traces them to Arawak speakers of the central Amazon Basin who moved down the <b>Orinoco</b> Valley and along the Lesser Antilles; the other, the circum-Caribbean theory of Julian H. Steward, derives them from the Colombian Andes.</blockquote><a href="/culture/taino">Taíno · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Columbus sighted Trinidad in 1498, but Spain made no lasting settlement until 1592, when Antonio de Berrío founded San José de Oruña as a base for searching for El Dorado on the <b>Orinoco</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/spanish-trinidad">Spanish Trinidad</a></li></ul></section>
